# Config for `npm run dev`. Deliberately the same three values as
# `.env.production`: there is no dev instance of BB PM, so local development
# talks to the production backend and the passcode flow, the OAuth flow and
# the comment data all behave exactly as they do deployed.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, READ THIS ONCE
#
# A comment you post while developing is a REAL comment in the SRM project.
# Everyone with the link sees it, and if you were signed in with BB PM it is
# an internal thread carrying your name. Delete your test threads when you're
# done — the trash icon in the rail is enough.
#
# Duplicated rather than shared through a plain `.env` because Vercel's build
# does not read `.env` — see the header of `.env.production`, which is where
# that was learned the expensive way. If you change a value here, change it
# there too.
#
# To point at a local API instead, put your overrides in `.env.local`
# (gitignored) — those win over this file. See `.env.example`.
#
# Sign-in works from here against production BB PM with no extra setup: the
# OAuth client is registered for `http://localhost:5173/oauth/callback`, the
# API lets any localhost origin through CORS on its own, and localhost counts
# as a secure context so PKCE's `crypto.subtle` is available. The port must be
# 5173 — hence `--strictPort` in the dev script, so a busy port fails loudly
# instead of drifting to 5174 where the redirect URI isn't registered.

VITE_BBPM_API_BASE=https://pm.burningbros.kr/api
VITE_BBPM_SHARE_TOKEN=16574965e46951bb100306f6cc73fd52
VITE_BBPM_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=bbpm_client_c6bed652395b0b7fe35a97023e0c097a
